.TH UFSRVRXLIB 7 "October 2026" "ufsrvrxlib 0.2.0" "UFSRV Programmer's Manual"
.\" ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
.SH NAME
ufsrvrxlib \- ufsrv reactive-extensions library (coroutines, futures, scheduler)
.\" ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include <ufsrvrxlib/ufsrv_future/ufsrv_future.h>
.B #include <ufsrvrxlib/ufsrv_coroutine/ufsrv_coroutine.h>
.B #include <ufsrvrxlib/ufsrv_scheduler/ufsrv_scheduler.h>
.B #include <ufsrvrxlib/ufsrv_cancellation/ufsrv_cancellation.h>
.B #include <ufsrvrxlib/version.h>
.fi
.sp
Link with
.BR \-lufsrvrxlib .
.\" ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B ufsrvrxlib
is the reactive-extensions library for the ufsrv messaging-server platform.
It provides asynchronous-programming primitives in the style of RxJava /
ListenableFuture / CompletableFuture, implemented in C17, as a four-layer
cake:

.RS 4
.PD 0
.TP 4
.B \(bu L1
API user code \(em fluent chains or await-style reads.
.TP
.B \(bu L2
.B Futures / promises
\(em composable, non-blocking result delivery.
.TP
.B \(bu L3
.B Coroutines
\(em stackful suspend/resume (a thin wrapper over libaco).
.TP
.B \(bu L4
.B Scheduler / threads
\(em worker threads over a lock-free MPSC queue and an eventfd.
.PD
.RE

Each layer depends only on the layer beneath it; the scheduler sees only
opaque job callbacks, never result semantics or coroutine stacks.  The
library consumes uflib (system-installed) and exposes a single static
library.
.\" ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
.SH MODULES
.\" ===================================================
.SS Scheduler \&(L4) \(em \fBufsrv_scheduler\fP
Single-worker schedulers and a round-robin pool.  A job is an opaque callback
run on a worker thread; submission is multi-producer, lock-free.
.PP
.TP
.B UfsrvSchedulerCreate(name)
Create a single-worker scheduler (not started).
.TP
.B UfsrvSchedulerStart(s)
Start the worker thread.
.TP
.B UfsrvSchedulerSubmit(s, cb, ctx)
Enqueue a job.
.TP
.B UfsrvSchedulerStop(s)
Signal the worker to stop (does not wait).
.TP
.B UfsrvSchedulerJoin(s)
Wait for the worker to exit.
.TP
.B UfsrvSchedulerDestroy(s)
Stop, join, drain and free.
.TP
.B UfsrvSchedulerIsWorkerThread(s)
Test whether the calling thread is the worker.
.PP
Pool variants mirror these and add
.BR UfsrvSchedulerPoolCreate ,
.BR UfsrvSchedulerPoolStart ,
.BR UfsrvSchedulerPoolSubmit ,
.BR UfsrvSchedulerPoolSubmitTo
(pinned to a worker),
.BR UfsrvSchedulerPoolStop ,
.BR UfsrvSchedulerPoolJoin ,
.BR UfsrvSchedulerPoolDestroy ,
and
.BR UfsrvSchedulerPoolSize .
.PP
Two introspection helpers describe a pool without linking json-c:
.TP
.B DescribeScheduler(pool, worker_name, bd)
Render the pool and its workers as JSON into a BufferDescriptor; a non-NULL
.I worker_name
selects one exact worker.  Pass a pre-initialised descriptor, or NULL to have one
allocated (256-byte initial capacity).
.TP
.B ListSchedulerWorkers(pool)
Every worker name, in pool order, carved from one allocation; release the returned
CollectionDescriptor with a single
.BR free (3).
.\" ===================================================
.SS Coroutines \&(L3) \(em \fBufsrv_coroutine\fP
Stackful coroutines: a body runs until it
.IR yields
or
.IR exits ,
and is resumed later on the same thread.  Coroutines are spawned onto a
scheduler; a suspended coroutine is re-submitted to its worker for resumption.
.PP
.TP
.B UfsrvCoroutineCreate(entry, arg)
Create a coroutine bound to the calling thread.
.TP
.B UfsrvCoroutineResume(co)
Run until it yields or exits (destroys on exit).
.TP
.B UfsrvCoroutineYield()
Suspend, returning to the resumer.
.TP
.B UfsrvCoroutineCurrent()
The currently running coroutine.
.TP
.B UfsrvCoroutineGetArg()
The entry argument.
.TP
.B UfsrvCoroutineCurrentScheduler()
The scheduler the coroutine is pinned to (NULL if none).
.TP
.B UfsrvCoroutineIsInCoroutine()
Test whether the caller is inside a coroutine rather than the thread's main
coroutine.
.TP
.B UfsrvCoroutineExit()
Terminate the coroutine (does not return).  A plain
.B return
from the entry does the same thing.
.TP
.B UfsrvCoroutineThreadCleanup()
Release the calling thread's coroutine state, reclaiming any coroutine created on
this thread but never resumed (its handle is invalid afterwards).  Coroutines that
have been resumed must have exited first; the state is also released at thread exit
by a TLS destructor.
.TP
.B UfsrvCoroutineSubmitResume(s, co)
Re-submit a suspended coroutine for resumption.
.TP
.B UfsrvCoroutineSpawn(s, entry, arg)
Create and first-resume a coroutine on a worker.
.\" ===================================================
.SS Futures and promises \&(L2) \(em \fBufsrv_future\fP
A promise is the one-shot producer; the future is the consumer.  Completion
runs registered continuations on the completing thread, over a lock-free
Treiber stack.  Operators either
.I borrow
the result (const, read-only) or
.I move
it (copy-then-zero), so a value is freed exactly once.
.PP
.TP
.B UfsrvPromiseCreate(&future)
Create a linked promise/future pair.
.TP
.B UfsrvPromiseSetValue(p, v, free)
Fulfil with a success value (ownership of
.I v
transferred).  Does
.I not
free the promise.
.TP
.B UfsrvPromiseSetError(p, err)
Fulfil with an error.  Does not free the promise.
.TP
.B UfsrvPromiseSetResult(p, result)
Fulfil with a full result.  Does not free the promise.
.TP
.B UfsrvPromiseDestroy(p)
Release the promise, fulfilled or not.  Destroying an
.I unfulfilled
promise completes its future with
.BR EPIPE .
.TP
.B UfsrvFutureThen(f, cb, ctx)
Register a terminal (borrow) continuation.
.TP
.B UfsrvFutureIsReady(f)
Query completion.
.TP
.B UfsrvFutureGet(f)
Await the result: suspends a coroutine, blocks a plain thread on an eventfd.  On a
scheduler worker thread outside a coroutine it refuses, returning
.BR EDEADLK
(asserting in debug builds), because blocking there would park the worker.
.TP
.B UfsrvFutureGetWithCancellation(f, token)
Await, honouring a cancellation token; returns
.B ECANCELED
if it is already cancelled, and is woken if it is cancelled during the wait.
.TP
.B UfsrvFutureAttachCancellation(f, token)
Bind a token so cancel completes the future with
.BR ECANCELED .
.TP
.B UfsrvFutureFromValue(v, free)
An already-completed successful future.
.TP
.B UfsrvFutureFromError(err)
An already-completed failed future.
.TP
.B UfsrvFutureMap(f, mapper, ctx)
Synchronous transform (borrow; new value, released with
.BR free (3)).
.TP
.B UfsrvFutureMapEx(f, mapper, ctx, free_value)
As
.BR UfsrvFutureMap ,
but the mapper's value is released with
.IR free_value .
Pass NULL to keep ownership in the caller.  Use it whenever the mapper returns a
pooled, arena-backed or refcounted object.
.TP
.B UfsrvFutureFlatMap(f, mapper, ctx)
Chain another asynchronous operation (move).
.TP
.B UfsrvFutureOnError(f, recover, ctx)
Recover from an error.
.TP
.B UfsrvFutureDoOnSuccess(f, action, ctx)
Run a success-only side effect, then forward.
.TP
.B UfsrvFutureAll(futures, n)
Wait for all (fail-fast).
.TP
.B UfsrvFutureZip(a, b, zipper, ctx)
Combine two; the combined value is released with
.BR free (3).
.TP
.B UfsrvFutureZipEx(a, b, zipper, ctx, free_value)
As
.BR UfsrvFutureZip ,
with an explicit destructor for the combined value.
.TP
.B UfsrvFutureAny(futures, n)
Complete with the first to finish.
.TP
.B UfsrvFutureRetain(f) / UfsrvFutureRelease(f)
Reference counting.
.\" ===================================================
.SS Cancellation \(em \fBufsrv_cancellation\fP
Cooperative cancellation tokens.  A token keeps a mutex-guarded intrusive list of
one-shot callbacks;
.B UfsrvCancellationTokenCancel
detaches and runs them on the cancelling thread, waking any waiter
(resume-on-cancel).  Cancellation is never pre-emptive: it is observed at yield
points.
.PP
.TP
.B UfsrvCancellationTokenCreate()
Create a token.
.TP
.B UfsrvCancellationTokenCancel(token)
Mark cancelled and run the registered callbacks.
.TP
.B UfsrvCancellationTokenIsCancelled(token)
Query the token.
.TP
.B UfsrvCancellationTokenRegister(token, cb, ctx)
Register a one-shot callback.
.TP
.B UfsrvCancellationTokenRegisterEx(token, cb, ctx, release)
Register with a context-release hook.
.TP
.B UfsrvCancellationTokenUnregister(handle)
Claim the callback so a later Cancel will not run it, drop the registrant's
reference and reclaim the node; returns false if Cancel had already run it.  The
handle is
.I single-use
and is dead afterwards \(em a second call with it dereferences freed memory.  Calling
this exactly once per handle is
.IR mandatory :
.B UfsrvCancellationTokenCancel
detaches a node before dispatching it, so a registration that is never unregistered
is never reclaimed, not even by
.BR UfsrvCancellationTokenDestroy ,
which walks a list that no longer contains it.
.TP
.B UfsrvCancellationTokenDestroy(token)
Release the caller's reference to the token and reclaim the nodes still on its list.
.\" ===================================================
.SS Lock-free Treiber stack \(em \fBlockless_treiber_stack\fP
The reusable intrusive lock-free LIFO stack the future layer builds on, now
provided by uflib (include
.BR <uflib/cdt/lockless_treiber_stack/lockless_treiber_stack.h> )
as an opaque handle: a multi-producer push and a single-consumer steal-all
drain, with per-node exactly-once
.IR claim
and
.IR release .
.PP
.TP
.B lockless_treiber_stack_create()
.TP
.B lockless_treiber_stack_destroy(stack)
.TP
.B lockless_treiber_stack_node_init(node)
.TP
.B lockless_treiber_stack_push(stack, node)
.TP
.B lockless_treiber_stack_steal_all(stack)
.TP
.B lockless_treiber_stack_claim(node)
.TP
.B lockless_treiber_stack_release(node)
.\" ===================================================
.SS Version \(em \fBversion\fP
.TP
.B UfsrvRxLibVersion()
Full dotted version string.
.TP
.B UfsrvRxLibVersionMajor() / Minor() / Patch()
Individual components.
.\" ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
.SH OWNERSHIP AND LIFETIME
.TP
.B One-shot promise
The first
.B UfsrvPromiseSet*
wins, and none of them frees the promise \(em always call
.BR UfsrvPromiseDestroy ,
fulfilled or not.  Destroying an unfulfilled promise completes its future with
.BR EPIPE ,
so an abandoned promise is reported rather than left hanging.
.TP
.B The future owns the value
Freed at reference-count zero via its
.I free_value
hook.  The one value the library produces rather than receives is a mapper's or
zipper's return: that is released with
.BR free (3)
by
.BR UfsrvFutureMap / UfsrvFutureZip ,
so those callbacks may only return a
.B malloc
block.  Use
.B UfsrvFutureMapEx / UfsrvFutureZipEx
to supply the destructor for anything else, or pass NULL to keep ownership.
.B UfsrvFutureGet
and
.B UfsrvFutureThen
hand back a
.I borrowed
value: read it, do not free it.
.TP
.B Borrow vs move
.I map
borrows (produces a new value);
.IR flatMap ,
.IR onError ,
and
.I doOnSuccess
move it (the input future no longer owns it).
.TP
.B Reference counting
.B UfsrvPromiseCreate
returns the future with count 2 (promise + caller); release your reference
with
.BR UfsrvFutureRelease .
.\" ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
.SH CANCELLATION MODEL
Cancellation is cooperative and observed, never pre-empted.
.B UfsrvFutureGetWithCancellation
returns
.B ECANCELED
if the token is cancelled before or during the wait (a suspended coroutine is
woken; a plain thread's eventfd is written).
.B UfsrvFutureAttachCancellation
completes the future with
.B ECANCELED
when the token fires, and single-input operators (map, flatMap, onError,
doOnSuccess) inherit the token onto their output \(em flatMap also onto the future its
mapper returns.  The future retains itself and holds its own reference on the token
until it completes, so a cancel after the future is released is still safe.
.PP
A registration node is reclaimed the moment its handle is consumed, so the rule for
raw
.B UfsrvCancellationTokenRegister
users is: consume every handle exactly once with
.BR UfsrvCancellationTokenUnregister ,
whether or not Cancel already ran the callback.  And always
.BR UfsrvCancellationTokenDestroy
your own token reference.
.\" ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
.SH RETURN VALUES
Functions returning
.I bool
report success (non-NULL arguments, allocation success, acceptance by a running
scheduler).  A future result carries
.IR error
and a borrowed
.IR value ;
the codes are:
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B ECANCELED
The token was cancelled.
.TP
.B EPIPE
Broken promise \(em the promise was destroyed unfulfilled.
.TP
.B EINVAL
A move operator found its input already consumed, or
.B UfsrvFutureAll
was handed a NULL input.
.TP
.B EDEADLK
A blocking get was attempted on a scheduler worker thread.
.TP
.B \-1
Allocation failure, a NULL argument, or a mapper/zipper that returned NULL.
.PP
Operators propagate the first error downstream; map, flatMap and zip never run
their callbacks on an error input.
.\" ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
.SH EXAMPLE
.nf
static void
download_entry(void)
{
    UfsrvPromise *p = UfsrvCoroutineGetArg();

    UfsrvPromiseSetValue(p, produce_result(), free);  /* completes the future */
    UfsrvPromiseDestroy(p);                           /* Set* does not free it */
}

int
main(void)
{
    UfsrvScheduler *s = UfsrvSchedulerCreate("worker");
    UfsrvSchedulerStart(s);

    UfsrvFuture *f = NULL;
    UfsrvPromise *p = UfsrvPromiseCreate(&f);
    UfsrvFutureThen(f, on_done, NULL);            /* observe completion */

    UfsrvCoroutineSpawn(s, download_entry, p);    /* do the work asynchronously */
    UfsrvFutureResult r = UfsrvFutureGet(f);      /* block/suspend until ready */
    if (r.error == 0) { use(r.value); }           /* borrowed: do not free */

    UfsrvFutureRelease(f);
    UfsrvSchedulerStop(s);
    UfsrvSchedulerJoin(s);
    UfsrvSchedulerDestroy(s);
    return 0;
}
.fi
See also the runnable programs under
.IR examples/ .
